# EMCOMM Field Node — Pre-Deployment Checklist

Items that cannot be verified by the provisioner and must be confirmed against
real hardware and your group's plan before a node is considered deployable.

## 1. Callsign and License
The callsign entered at provisioning is written into the JS8Call profile as MyCall
and into the Direwolf config as MYCALL.

Confirm:
    - the callsign is real, current, and belongs to the operator of THIS node
    - the operator holds the class of license required for the modes enabled
    - no node ships with a placeholder still in place

Verify with:
    grep -i mycall ~/.config/JS8Call.ini ~/.config/direwolf/direwolf.conf

## 2. Profile Contamination (configs/*.ini, configs/*.conf)
A profile captured from a working install carries that machine's identity.
Before distributing any profile, check for:
    - a real callsign         (MyCall / MYCALL)
    - a grid square           (MyGrid)
    - absolute home paths     (/home/<someone>/...)
    - hardcoded audio devices (a specific card or PCI path)

Verify with:
    grep -nE "MyCall|MyGrid|/home/|alsa_|plughw|pci-" configs/*.ini configs/*.conf

Replace identity with MYCALL_PLACEHOLDER, paths with HOME_PLACEHOLDER, and leave
grid and audio device fields EMPTY for the operator to set per node.

## 3. Audio Interface (JS8Call, Direwolf)
Sound-card interface device names differ per machine and per adapter, and USB
audio enumeration order is not stable across reboots.

Verify with the interface connected:
    arecord -l
    pactl list short sinks
    pactl list short sources

Set the result in JS8Call -> File -> Settings -> Audio, and in ADEVICE in
direwolf.conf. Consider a udev rule to pin a stable name if the index moves.

## 4. PTT Keying (Direwolf)
The shipped Direwolf config uses PTT CM108, which is common for USB sound-card
interfaces but has documented reliability issues on Linux.

Confirm PTT actually keys the radio, into a dummy load, before relying on it.
If CM108 fails, a serial RTS/DTR PTT interface is the usual fallback.

## 5. Operating Area Map Tiles (scripts/fetch_map_tiles.py)
Selecting the map step adds an Operating Area screen: a center point in decimal
degrees and a radius of 50, 75 or 150 miles. It writes configs/areas/<node>-area.json
and shows the tile count, size and time before anything downloads.

Tiles are tiered — full street detail within 25 miles, orientation zoom to the
full radius. Coverage scales with the square of the radius, so a flat fetch at
street zoom across 150 miles would be roughly 694,000 tiles against a public
USGS endpoint.

Do not ship one group's area to another group, and do not commit an area you
would rather not publish; configs/areas/*.json is gitignored for that reason.

Confirm:
    - the center and radius cover your actual response area
    - the estimate fits the disk you have, before starting the download
    - tiles land under ~/EMCOMM_Data/Offline_Maps/Offline_Tiles/<layer>/
    - no leftover configs/areas/example-area.json is present from an older clone

## 6. Time Sync Behavior
Digital modes are timing-sensitive; JS8Call in particular needs the clock close
to correct. The dock-trigger autostart waits up to 15s for chrony to report a
normal leap status, then continues regardless.

Verify actual cold-start sync time:
    chronyc tracking
Adjust the wait ceiling if your GPS takes longer to acquire.

## 7. Radio Interface Enumeration Timing
Autostart waits up to 10s for /dev/ttyUSB* or /dev/ttyACM* before launching the
digital-mode applications. Confirm your interface enumerates within that window
after a cold boot, or raise the ceiling.

## 8. Dock Trigger (Havis DS-PAN-111 + CF-30 only)
The udev rule matches the Havis DS-PAN-111 dock's USB hub, 05e3:0610. That is a
real ID, not a placeholder — it is the reference hardware this package targets.

On any other dock it will not match, the rule never fires, and nothing
autostarts on dock. Leave the step unchecked.

To port it to different hardware, find your own dock's IDs:
    udevadm monitor --environment --udev
(then dock/undock and read the actual idVendor/idProduct)

Update 99-dock-trigger.rules with those values. That is a port, and verifying it
is on you.

## 9. Meshtastic Net Parameters
Region, preset and channel must match across every node in the group or they
will not hear each other. See the staged reference at
~/EMCOMM_Data/Meshtastic/meshtastic_setup.md.

Confirm the group has agreed region, preset and channel name before deployment,
and decide per node whether position reporting should be on.

## 10. Offline Knowledgebase Currency
The Kiwix ZIM download is pinned to a specific dated file and checksum. That URL
will eventually 404 as upstream rotates snapshots.

If the download or checksum fails, pick a current file from
https://download.kiwix.org/zim/ and update the URL and SHA-256 in the script.

## 11. Unattended Updates
If the "appliance build" step was run, unattended updates are disabled. That is
correct for an imaged node kept offline, and wrong for a laptop in daily use.

Either way: patch the node manually before each deployment cycle.

## 12. SatDump First Launch (TLEs)
**Only if the SatDump step was run. Do this while the node still has network.**

SatDump writes its runtime configuration and fetches TLEs on **first launch of the
GUI**, not at install time — its own setting is described as "auto update happens at
launch only". A node that has never had SatDump opened arrives in the field with no
element sets and cannot predict a pass.

Open SatDump once, confirm the Tracking tab lists satellites, and close it.

**Repeat this shortly before the node deploys.** Element sets decay within days and
degrade badly past two weeks — a stale TLE does not fail loudly, it points the dish
at empty sky. A node imaged months before deployment needs a fresh fetch regardless
of what was captured at provisioning time.

Nothing shipped in this repository substitutes for it: no TLE set is bundled, because
a file committed to git is stale the day it is committed. A curated set for the
handful of birds a node actually works can be staged as `configs/satdump_tles.txt`,
supplied by the group like every other profile — but it still needs refreshing.

Which of those two arrangements the node got decides what first launch does:

* **No `configs/satdump_tles.txt`** (the default — `configs/` ships empty). The
  provisioner leaves SatDump's own fetch enabled and first launch pulls its full
  default set, upwards of 16,000 objects. This is the normal case.
* **A curated set supplied.** The provisioner stages it and switches SatDump's fetch
  off, because the fetch would otherwise overwrite the staged file on first launch.
  Element sets then only ever come from that file, so refreshing it is entirely the
  group's responsibility.

Either way the Tracking tab is the check: open SatDump, confirm it lists satellites.
A node showing `0 TLEs loaded!` in the log has neither — stop and find out which
half is missing before it deploys.

## 13. Full Dry Run
Before the node counts as ready, exercise it end to end:
    - transmit and receive on each enabled mode, into a dummy load or on a net
    - confirm maps render offline with the network cable pulled
    - confirm the document server answers at http://127.0.0.1:8085
    - run the machine on battery for the duration of an expected shift

## 14. GPS Time Source (gpsd + chrony)
Without this a node's only time sources are internet NTP pools it cannot reach
in the field. It never synchronises, `chronyc tracking` reports
`Not synchronised` indefinitely, and nothing reports an error. JS8Call feels it
first — its timed transmit windows want the clock inside about a second, and a
node that has been off for a week can be far enough out to hear nothing and be
heard by nobody, with silence as the only symptom.

Verified end to end on the sibling project's command node with a GlobalSat
BU-353N: cold boot, no network, GPS selected at stratum 1 within about a
minute. Not yet run on an EmComm node.

**Naming the receiver.** Copy `configs/gps.conf.sample` to `configs/gps.conf`
and set `DEVICE` to a by-id path:

    ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/          # with the receiver attached

Use by-id, not `/dev/ttyUSB0` — numbering moves as soon as a radio interface
is attached. `BAUD` is optional but worth setting when known; gpsd will
otherwise hunt for the speed, and on the receiver this was built against it
failed to find 4800 within fifteen seconds.

**The provisioner never probes for a receiver.** Opening a serial port asserts
DTR, and some CAT interfaces key PTT on DTR or RTS, so sweeping `ttyUSB*`
hunting for a GPS could put a radio on the air. You name the device.

**Four things that each break this silently**, all handled by the step but
worth knowing if you configure a node by hand:
    - `gpsd.service` is not enabled on a stock node; only `gpsd.socket` is,
      and socket activation starts gpsd when a *client* connects. chrony reads
      shared memory and never connects, so gpsd never runs.
    - `-n`, or gpsd does not poll until a client connects. Same outcome.
    - `-b`, or gpsd probes the device by *writing* to it. That locked up a
      BU-353N completely: port open, correct speed, zero bytes.
    - `prefer` on the refclock, or chronyd keeps steering from an
      **unreachable** NTP server for hours after the network drops, because a
      stale source's dispersion grows at only ~1 ppm.

chrony is restarted before gpsd, because chronyd owns the shared-memory
segment gpsd attaches to.

**Confirming it:**

    chronyc tracking | grep -E 'Reference ID|Stratum|Leap'

Expect `Reference ID : … (GPS0)`, `Stratum : 1`, `Leap status : Normal`.
Acquisition takes roughly a minute from cold; before that `chronyc sources`
shows `GPS0` with reach `0`, which is normal rather than a fault.

**A USB puck may reset when the port closes.** The BU-353N restarts when DTR
drops and needs ~10 seconds to resume. Anything that opens and closes the port
costs the node ten seconds of time source — which is why the verification row
asks chronyd rather than opening the device.

## 15. Operator's Manual
The node can serve its own manual, and a node that needs the manual is usually a
node with no network to fetch it over. This costs one download and one file copy,
and it has to happen *before* the run — the document server step copies whatever is
in `docs/` at that moment and does not come back for more.

Download `EmComm-Operators-Manual.pdf` from the release page and put it in `docs/`:

    cp ~/Downloads/EmComm-Operators-Manual.pdf docs/

Or build it from the markdown source that ships in the tarball:

    python3 scripts/build_manual.py          # needs reportlab

Either way, confirm it landed after provisioning:

    ls ~/EMCOMM_Data/PDF_Manuals/

and that it is reachable at `http://127.0.0.1:8085` with the network pulled. A run
with an empty `docs/` warns rather than failing, so an absent manual is easy to miss
until the node is somewhere you cannot fix it.

## 16. Hardware
A recommended and tested hardware list ships with the release, covering
everything except radios. Two things about it:

**Tested means tested.** An entry on that list has been run on a node. Anything
present but unproven is named as such rather than implied to work.

**The DigiRig audio/PTT interface is NOT among the tested items.** Direwolf's
config ships with `ADEVICE plughw:1,0` and `PTT CM108` as placeholders, both
flagged in the file itself, and CM108-style PTT over `/dev/hidraw*` has
documented reliability problems on Linux. Nothing in this repository has keyed
a radio. Watch the radio key before relying on the station to transmit — see
section 8 and the Direwolf chapter of the operator's manual.
